Date: Thu, 18 Nov 93 09:39:40 EST
From: Tim Freeman <tsf@cs.cmu.edu>
Subject: Meta model and E-prime

From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
>someone who pointed out in a private message that in "is written",
>"is" only functions as an auxiliary verb forming the passive voice of
>"written", and thus need not be considered a violation of the rules of
>E-prime.

I read about E-prime in Etc. a while back, and they claimed that in
practice, people cannot only use "to be" in only certain grammatical
contexts, because the habit they need to develop to stop themselves
from using the verb must be simple.  [Any ideas about how to put the
previous sentence in E-prime while keeping the word "simple" at the
end for emphasis?]

A related system is the meta-model by Bandler and Grinder, as defined
in The Structure of Magic, volume 1, chapter 4.  [Using "to be" to get
the emphasis right again.]  The meta-model is a procedure for mapping
an utterance into a set of questions for grammatically clarifying the
utterance.  [I could translate the previous sentence into e-prime by
starting "They define the meta-model as...", but it seems redundant to
say again that B&G defined the meta-model.]  The MM points to many
forms of ambiguity that are not filled in by translating to E-prime.
["To be" as auxiliary verb.]  In practice, if one asks all the
questions generated by the meta-model, the conversation bogs down
hopelessly; on the other hand, one learns a lot by meta-modeling one's
own internal dialogue to the point where one knows the answer to all
of the questions generated.  [Note that the previous sentence is in
e-prime, but the referent of "one" is ambiguous; the meta-model would
have generated the question "Who do you mean by 'one'?" among many
others.]  The interesting case happens when the meta-model generates a
question that has *no* answer, thus showing that the original
utterance was, in some sense, malformed.  ["To be" to get the emphasis
right, again.  Also, I'm defining a word that I'll use in the next
sentence.]  So far as I know, I have eliminated the thought habits
that lead to such malformed utterances in my own internal dialogue; in
my opinion the project was worthwhile.  ["To be" for emphasis again.]

As the above square brackets show, having to rationalize every use of
"to be" is a pain in the ass.  As readers of this can perhaps tell, I
think speaking and writing E-prime isn't a very good idea; it is
obtrusive (the writer in Etc. had people asking him what his native
language was when it was *English*!), and the meta-model seems to me
to be a better mental hygeine technique.

Tim

