Kennita Watson writes:

> For the initiated, a request:  please help me out by translating the
> following into E-prime -- the book gives a definition, but very few
> examples, and I don't see how to do these.
>	1) "[The book] is written in E-prime."
>	2) "What are you doing?"


The first one's easy:

(1) The author wrote the book in E-prime.

In general, it's a good idea to avoid the passive voice ("is written"), 
as it obscures the identity of the person/animal/thing that performed 
an action. That's a major advantage of E-prime, in which the passive voice
is eliminated by the prohibtion on "be".

The second one is harder, if not impossible.  The reason is that "be" is
used as an auxiliary verb to express progressive aspect.  In this usage,
it is no more epistemologically harmful than "have" and "will".  ("He has
left already"; "Will you go to the movies tonight?")  Hard-core E-primers
shun *all* usage of "be", but I see this as unnecessary.

>I would also appreciate a general rule for tranlating sentences in
>progressive tenses, such as "I am going to the store.", "I was eating
>lunch.", etc.

See the paragraph immediately above.  Aspect (i.e., whether an action was
ongoing or not) is such an essential feature of English that I don't think
a general rule is possible.


>I also note my fascination with Heidegger's introduction to _Being and
>Time_, and my delight in twisting myself into mental pretzels over
>such sentences as "Insofar as Being constitutes what is asked about,
>and insofar as Being means the Being of beings, beings themselves turn
>out to be what is _interrogated_ in the question of Being.".  Not to
>mention that to ask "What is Being?" in a precise way requires already
>knowing what Being is in some sense, because we need to know what it
>would mean for Being to _be_ anything. 

To which I reply "Mu."  

>I would also
>welcome opinions/discussion of the value of General Semantics and/or
>Rational Emotive Therapy (I think both fit under the umbrella of Self
>Transformation, and possibly Dynamic Optimism, if you wonder what
>business they have on the Extropians list).

I like General Semantics, which tries to get people to express themselves
(and thereby to think) more clearly and precisely.  My mother likes Rational
Emotive Therapy and has sent me some books on it, but I didn't have much success
trying to read them.  I either found the statements obvious, or I disagreed with 
them.  John Bradshaw (the "inner child" guy) has criticized these kinds of
cognitive approaches on the grounds that emotion, and not thought, are primary
in human development, and I tend to agree with him.  I think that R.E.T. is
good for people like my Mom who are generally mentally healthy and suffer occasional
periods of sadness.  For people like me, who are prone to dysthymia or clinical 
depression, R.E.T. probably won't do anymore than someone saying "Cheer up!"

