From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Drugs: questions/discussion

First, let me say that I have been interested in the effects of
various psychoactive chemicals for a long time; however, if I
have any personal experience with using any illegal ones, I'm
not likely to go on an on about it in a public forum such as
this.  I take Tim's BlackNet Investigations scenario quite
seriously.  I will say that as a good Extropian I favor
decriminalization of all substances, and that someday I might
try some of them if I am convinced by my research that the
risks are appropriate -- risks both from the chemicals themselves
and from societal reaction to their use.  I have lots of time
to complete such research.  The information I present here is
easily available in various books, and the "street" knowledge
comes from intense questioning and observation of friends with
firsthand knowledge, usually gained years ago.  I find it interesting
that I don't even know anybody who has "current" experience with
stronger psychoactive drugs... if I did want to take some now, I'm
not even sure how I would find any!

Despite this, it seems to be fairly common for people to confess
to drug use on the Net, quite plainly, and as far as I know none
of them have suffered legal consequences for that.

Kennita Watson:
 
> Can one really buy LSD that cheap? [$3]

Yes, unless the stepped-up efforts of law enforcement have changed
things drastically recently.

> I assume that if one can, that the product has little or no QC.
> Can one get hi-Q LSD, and how much more would that cost?

The reason for the cheapness is the (relative) ease of manufacture
and the tremendous economies of scale.  A "dose" of LSD is typically
about 100 *micrograms*, and a "recipe" for manufacturing the drug
might typically produce a gram or more: 10,000 doses.

Purity is not much of an issue for LSD because at microgram levels,
none of the by-products are particularly dangerous (though I often
wonder about this -- after all, a single mutation by a carcinogenic
chemical can lead to full-blown cancer...)

> Does the dosage of LSD affect the length of the trip or its intensity
> or both?

Both.  A recently-retired member of the AI faculty at the local University
wrote a book in the 60's in which he did laboratory experiments on
students using LSD and (I think) mescaline and psilocybin.  It is a very
interesting book (author is Len Uhr, title forgotten).  A 50 microgram dose
of LSD had little effect; 500 micrograms produced very profound effects.
There are urban legends about people accidentally ingesting as much as
10 milligrams, reportedly increasing the duration and intensity immensely.

Note that Sandoz used to (and I believe still does) commercially produce
LSD for psychiatric use.  Presumably that (in pill form, I think) would
be as "pure" as you're likely to find.  Of course, you're not likely
to find it.

> How commonly does a group of friends get together for such an
> experience and have someone actually volunteer to be "ground control"?
> I would expect everyone to want to participate.

My understanding is that many people who use drugs such as LSD do so
in a fairly responsible manner.  In such a situation there is *always*
somebody with experience who will act as a guide, providing an
appropriately secure environment.  Many of these people sincerely
believe that drug experiences are not just thrills but are truly
useful tools in enhancing self-understanding, and should be taken
quite seriously.

> 	It's [MDMA] an empathogenic ego-softener, not a dissolver.
> 
> What does that mean?

An "empathogen" gives the subjective experience of breaking down
barriers between people.  LSD can obliterate the sense of self
entirely.

> Here I need that book on brain chemistry.  Quickly (?), what does
> serotonin do?

Serotonin is an inhibitory neurotransmitter.  Brown and Godwin, at
NIMH, have done studies that have correlated chronic low levels of
serotonin with destructive behavior (including suicide).  Hallucinogens
work (partly at least) by temporarily suppressing serotonin to a profound
extent, eliminating its inhibitory effects.  Dr. Arnold Mandell (I
think at UCSD) hypothesizes that the hippocampus is responsible for
mediating the interaction between sense data and internal processing.
Suppressing serotonin in the hippocampus could then have the effect
of making this mediation less effective, causing seeming "connections"
that wouldn't otherwise be formed, and allowing many aspects of
internal reality to reach a conscious level.  Serotonin activity can also
be dampened by prolonged exercise such as marathon running, fasting,
and perhaps even meditation.

> Does PIHKAL stand for anything?

Psychoactives (or maybe Psychedelics) I Have Known And Loved.  Shulgin
is about as far-out a "drug designer" as you're likely to find.

