From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: PHYSICS: Theory and experiment

I have a question for those (such as Mike Price) who know more
about physics than I; it relates to the discussion of whether
it's a good idea to build massive particle accelerators at
great expense.

If I understand correctly, certain very basic things are not
well understood.  One of these is the masses of various
particles.  In Science, when Physicists were moaning about
their jobs and the value of the houses they'd bought in
Texas, it was stated that the determination of the mass of
the top quark and the Higgs boson could help figure these
things out.  My question is: why?  Is it just a hope that
a couple of more data points will reveal a pattern, or is
there something more principled involved?

It seems to me that we already have lots of well-measured
masses to compare to theoretical predictions; how would
a couple more help?  Even putting aside questions about
whether the government should steal our money to fund
the projects, to me it makes more sense to delay them
until the answer will actually resolve something -- this
would require several competing theories which explain
the current data and make different predictions about
what the unknown values should be (and I mean *specific*
predictions... shouldn't theory be able to make specific
predictions?)  Until we have that, who cares?

Are there current theories that accurately account for
the masses of electrons, neutrons, and protons?  If
not, shouldn't we encourage much cheaper theoretical
efforts to do so?

derek


