I don't have the Official Listing of the Principles handy, so I'll
have to wing it.

"Socialism" already happens in many forms: families, communes,
households, etc. Sometimes it works, sometimes it doesn't. Children in
families are usually ready to leave by around age 18 or so.

"Socialism" as a political system, wherein the participation is not
voluntary and folks cannot freely leave the system, violates the
tenets of "spontaneous order" (voluntary self-association).

As long as it's voluntary, no problem. Like I said, many aspects of
our lives have strongly socialist aspects.

But when someone says "I am a socialist" (or "I am a Socialist") that
usually connotes a cloud of ideas associated with government-provided
health care and education, perhaps government-provided jobs, national
industrial policy, government ownership of the "means of production,"
and so on. Look at Socialist governments in France and Britain for
some examples.

These policies are generally not voluntary. A socialist government
does not allow some members to drop out of the system. The Hilary
Health Plan, for example, will throw me in jail, ultimately, if I do
not contribute to it, even though I do not desire its services.

The contradiction with Extropians ideas seems pretty clear.

Extropians have no problem with a bunch of folks organizing themselves
into a socialist household, a commune, or even a corporation (which usually
have simultaneously socialist, fascist, and communist elements running
through them). But Extropians object strongly to being coerced into
joining someone else's idea of utopia.

